.claude/plans/PLAN-193/repass-rc1/verdict-rc1-1.txt:13:Read-only verification passed migration and failure-path probes, version-floor boundary/timeout probes, template derivation, model parity, version consistency, Python 3.9 parsing, shell syntax and ShellCheck. The conditions digest matches. Filesystem writes were mocked; no full installation or live session was exercised. The worktree remains unchanged.
.claude/plans/PLAN-193/repass-ga/verdict-ga-4.txt:6:- **Declared debt:** condition **13(c)** accurately describes the extraction-path collision. Condition **14** accurately preserves the stray TODO marker and symlinked-parent defects. I found no escalation of these findings onto the mainline install/upgrade path.
.claude/plans/PLAN-193/repass-ga/verdict-ga-2.txt:6:- **Condição 4 — recuperação:** [`ceo-launches.py:290`](.claude/scripts/ceo-launches.py#L290) publica por hard link após escrita completa, `fsync` e fechamento. A limpeza opera sobre os nomes temporários. A segunda leitura do snapshot é conferida antes do cabeçalho de chamada exata (`:433`). Confiança no diretório, symlinks nos componentes pais, sobras temporárias, restrições do filesystem e ausência de garantia após queda de energia estão declaradas.
.claude/plans/PLAN-193/repass-ga/verdict-ga-1.txt:9:- Hook registrations, permissions and environment settings are unchanged in the three settings surfaces.
.claude/plans/PLAN-193/repass-ga/verdict-ga-1.txt:18:Read-only probes passed migration branches, preservation, write-failure handling, recovery-command quoting, version-floor boundaries and timeouts. Template derivation, model parity, env inventory, Python 3.9 parsing and shell syntax passed. ShellCheck reported informational diagnostics only. The conditions digest matches; the worktree is unchanged. Filesystem writes were mocked; no full installation or live harness session was exercised.
      # leaves the ORIGINAL run re-approvable (or its failed portion
      # re-runnable) with checkout pinned to the OLD event SHA — every
      # in-run version check still passes (both trees carry the same
      # version string), the registry is still empty, and npm publish
      # would irreversibly ship the WRONG tree while the live tag points
      # elsewhere. Fail-closed liveness assert: the CURRENT remote tag
      # must still peel to this run's GITHUB_SHA immediately before
      # publish. A deleted tag (empty resolve) and a moved tag both
      # abort. This step sits AFTER the production-npm environment
      # approval by job order, so a stale approval cannot race past it.
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------
      - name: Assert remote tag still points at this run's SHA
        # Same skip-condition as the publish step (rail rc.3 r1 P1-2): when
        # the registry already carries the version, publish is a no-op and
        # this guard must not turn the documented idempotent re-run red.
        if: steps.already_published.outputs.published != 'true'
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          TAG_REF="${GITHUB_REF_NAME}"
          # ONE ls-remote invocation = ONE remote snapshot (rail rc.3 r1
          # P1-1): separate calls could straddle a concurrent re-tag, keep
          # the OLD peeled SHA and approve the obsolete run.
          SNAP="$(git ls-remote origin "refs/tags/${TAG_REF}" "refs/tags/${TAG_REF}^{}")"
          PEELED="$(printf '%s\n' "$SNAP" | awk -v r="refs/tags/${TAG_REF}^{}" '$2==r{print $1}')"
          PLAIN="$(printf '%s\n' "$SNAP" | awk -v r="refs/tags/${TAG_REF}" '$2==r{print $1}')"
          LIVE="${PEELED:-$PLAIN}"
          if [ -z "$LIVE" ]; then
            echo "::error::tag ${TAG_REF} no longer exists on origin — refusing to publish an orphaned tree (delete/re-tag recovery in flight?)"
            exit 1
          fi
          if [ "$LIVE" != "${GITHUB_SHA}" ]; then
            echo "::error::tag ${TAG_REF} now peels to ${LIVE} but this run was built from ${GITHUB_SHA} — obsolete run; approve/re-run only from the CURRENT tag's run"
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "OK: refs/tags/${TAG_REF} still peels to ${GITHUB_SHA}"

      - name: Publish (Trusted Publishing — OIDC)
        if: steps.already_published.outputs.published != 'true'
        working-directory: npm
        # PLAN-158 W1: NO long-lived token — npm >=11.5.1 detects the
        # GitHub Actions OIDC context and mints a short-lived credential
        # against the npmjs trusted-publisher registration (repo
        # Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration, workflow npm-publish.yml,
        # environment production-npm). --provenance keeps working (same
        # OIDC JWT feeds Sigstore).
        #
        # ROLLBACK (token fallback) — kept dormant BY DESIGN, do not
        # inline-enable: apply
        # .claude/plans/PLAN-158/staged/wave1/rollback-oidc-to-token.patch
        # (restores the `env: NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` block below), regenerate a
        # granular NPM_TOKEN in the Owner console, then delete + re-tag
        # (tag runs pin the workflow to the tag's tree — a re-run of the
        # failed run would re-execute the OIDC tree). Full sequence:
        # .claude/plans/PLAN-158/oidc-failure-playbook.md
        run: npm publish --provenance --access public

      - name: Publish skipped — version already in registry (no-op success)
        if: steps.already_published.outputs.published == 'true'
        run: |
          echo "::notice::publish skipped — exact version already in the registry; this re-run is an idempotent no-op (PLAN-153 Wave B item 5)"

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  # PLAN-194 W4.3 — RC TOOLCHAIN PROOF. RC tags never reach `publish`
  # (its RC exclusion above is load-bearing and unchanged); they run
  # THIS job instead, so the toolchain the GA publish will use is
  # exercised before GA: the same Node line, the same exact npm, the
  # same staging + install.sh trailer + packlist gate, then a
  # `npm pack --dry-run` (no archive is written). Every step it shares
  # with `publish` is a BYTE-IDENTICAL copy — edit both, or
  # test_release_workflow_asserts.py (Plan194W4PublishToolchainTest)
  # goes red. Its only own steps are an RC-aware VERSION check (an RC
  # tree carries the GA VERSION — release.yml's RC flow) and the pack
  # dry-run.
  # Its permissions are `contents: read` ONLY: no OIDC token permission
  # (so no registry credential can be minted), no GitHub environment, no
  # `secrets.` reference, and no step that writes to the registry. The
  # runner's ephemeral job token (scoped by that `contents: read`) goes
  # only to the two pinned actions — checkout fetches with it, and
  # setup-node takes it as its default `token` input to download Node —
  # and is not left in .git/config for the steps that run repo code
  # (`persist-credentials: false` on the checkout). It does not wait
  # for `await-release-gate`: it changes nothing, and the toolchain
  # evidence should exist even on an RC whose gate goes red.
  # What it does NOT prove (declared residual, PLAN-194 OQ-5): the OIDC
  # token exchange and the registry write — both are first exercised at
  # GA, with .claude/plans/PLAN-158/oidc-failure-playbook.md as the net.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  rc-toolchain-proof:
    name: RC toolchain proof (no publish)
    if: contains(github.ref, '-rc.')
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 8
    permissions:
      contents: read
    steps:
      - name: Checkout tag
        # SHA-pinned (Sprint 7 Dependabot bump): actions/checkout@v6.0.2
        # persist-credentials: false (PLAN-194 W4): the job token is not
        # left in .git/config for the later steps, which run repo code
        # (install.sh smoke, npm pack). None of them needs it: the only
        # remote git call, publish's tag-liveness `git ls-remote origin`,
